course: biology
lesson: 305
title: Bees beyond the hive: bumblebees, solitary bees, carpenter bees and stingless bees
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When people say «bee» they often picture a honeybee hive, but there are about 20,000 kinds of bee and most of them live alone, each female building her own little nest.
explain: On a cold spring morning a fat furry bumblebee queen is often the first bee out. She shivers her flight muscles to warm up, and her fur keeps the heat in, so she can fly when honeybees stay at home.
explain: Look at a bee hotel of hollow stems in a garden: a red mason bee fills each stem with a row of rooms, each with pollen and one egg, walled off with mud. Leafcutter bees wrap their rooms in neat circles cut from rose leaves.
explain: Every bee has four wings, joined in flight by tiny hooks, and only females can sting, because the sting is a changed egg-laying tube. Some bees in warm countries can hardly sting at all and bite instead.
fact: Bumblebees shiver their flight muscles to warm up, and their fur keeps the heat in, so they fly in cool weather; queen bumblebees are often the first bees seen in early spring.
fact: The old story that scientists «proved» bumblebees cannot fly came from a rough sum treating a bee wing like a stiff aeroplane wing; real bee wings flap about 200 times a second and make tiny whirlwinds that give lift.
fact: In buzz pollination a bumblebee vibrates its muscles to shake pollen out of flowers like tomatoes, whose pollen is held in tubes; greenhouse tomato farmers often keep boxes of bumblebees.
fact: Bumblebee colonies are small, often a few dozen to a few hundred bees; only the new queens survive the winter, sleeping underground, and in spring each queen starts a new nest alone.
fact: Most of the world's 20,000 or so kinds of bee live alone; each female digs or builds her own nest and leaves food for each egg, and mining bees may nest close together like a village.
fact: Red mason bees fill hollow stems with a row of cells, each with pollen and an egg, sealed by mud walls; eggs that become males are laid nearest the door, and the front bees come out first in spring.
fact: Leafcutter bees cut neat circles from leaves to wrap each egg and its food like a parcel; they do not eat the leaves, and the plant barely notices.
fact: Carpenter bees chew tunnels in wood for their nests and push the sawdust out instead of eating it; the violet carpenter bee is one of Europe's biggest bees and has been reported spreading into Britain.
fact: Stingless bees live in warm countries like Brazil, Mexico and Australia; their stingers are tiny and useless, so they defend by biting and crowding intruders, and the Maya kept them for honey long before Europeans reached America.
fact: Only female bees have a sting, because the sting is a changed egg-laying tube; male bees cannot sting, and most solitary bees rarely sting, with mild stings.
fact: Bees have four wings, but a row of tiny hooks joins each front wing to the back wing in flight, so each pair works like one big wing.
exercise: How does a bumblebee warm up on a cold morning? | It shivers its flight muscles | It sits in a flower to sunbathe all day | It eats hot nectar from flowers | It rubs its legs hard on a stone
exercise: Why can a bumblebee fly in cool weather when honeybees stay home? | Its fur coat keeps the heat in | It has bigger wings than any bird | It never feels any cold at all, ever | It flies only inside greenhouses
exercise: Where did the idea that bumblebees «should not fly» come from? | A rough sum that treated its wing like a stiff aeroplane wing | A real experiment with a bee shut in a glass box, which could not take off | Bees that really cannot fly | A law passed by a scientist
exercise: About how many times a second do a bee's wings flap? | About 200 | About 2 | About 20,000 | About 20
exercise: What is buzz pollination? | A bee vibrating its muscles to shake pollen out of a flower | A bee humming a loud tune to call its friends to the flowers | A flower buzzing to attract bees | A bee biting a hole in a flower
exercise: Why do tomato farmers keep boxes of bumblebees in greenhouses? | Bumblebees shake pollen out of tomato flowers by buzzing | Bumblebees eat the greenfly and other pests on tomato leaves | Bumblebees make tomato honey | Bumblebees keep the greenhouse warm
exercise: Which bumblebees survive the winter? | Only the new queens, sleeping underground | The whole colony, packed together inside the hive | Only the males, which sleep inside old mouse nests | None, new bees grow out of the flowers in spring
exercise: How do most kinds of bee in the world live? | Alone, each female making her own nest | In huge hives of 50,000 bees with one queen | In pairs of mother and father | Inside other animals' nests
exercise: What does a red mason bee put in each room of its stem nest? | Pollen and one egg, sealed with mud | Honey and a hundred tiny eggs | Leaves and a queen | Sawdust and water from the pond
exercise: In a mason bee stem, why do the bees nearest the door come out first? | So nobody behind them gets stuck | Because they are the oldest queens | Because the door bees are just lazy | Because they need the most sunshine
exercise: Why do leafcutter bees cut circles out of leaves? | To wrap each egg and its food like a parcel | To eat as their main food all through the summer | To make little sails for flying on windy days | To mark the way home to the nest for their sisters
exercise: What does a carpenter bee do with the wood it chews out? | Pushes the sawdust out of its tunnel | Eats it for dinner, like a termite does | Turns it into sweet honey for the winter | Feeds it to its hungry larvae in the nest
exercise: How do stingless bees defend their nest? | By biting and crowding intruders | With a very strong sting | By spraying hot liquid | They never defend it at all
exercise: Who kept stingless bees for honey long before Europeans reached America? | The Maya | The Vikings | The ancient Greeks | Nobody, they make no honey
exercise: Why can a male bee never sting you? | The sting is a changed egg-laying tube that only females have | Male bees lose their sting every winter and grow it back in spring | Male bees are too small to sting | Male bees sting only at night
exercise: Why does a flying bee seem to have only two wings? | Tiny hooks join each front wing to the back wing | Two of its wings fall off in the middle of summer | It folds two wings under its body | Bees only ever have two wings
test: Count them: 3 bees land on a flower. How many wings is that? | 12 | 6 | 9 | 18
test: Count them: a bee hotel has 4 stems with 6 rooms in each, one egg per room. How many eggs? | 24 | 10 | 12 | 46
test: Count them: 2 bumblebees and 3 mason bees sit on a wall. How many legs is that altogether? | 30 | 20 | 15 | 40
test: «my cousin says science proved bumblebees can't fly lol» What is the best answer? | That was a wrong sum about stiff wings; real bee wings flap fast and stir the air | Yes, they float by magic | Yes, they only fly because nobody told them they can't, it's true | They glide only and never flap
test: In Bee Movie, the hero is a male bee who could sting people. What is true? | Only female bees have a sting; males cannot sting | All bees sting, male or female, if they are cross | Only males can sting | No bee can ever sting
test: In a cartoon, Winnie-the-Pooh finds a honeybee hive in a tree. Which bees would you find in a hollow stem in a bee hotel instead? | Solitary bees like mason bees | A whole honeybee colony | Bumblebee queens in winter | Stingless bees from Brazil
test: You find neat round holes cut from your rose leaves. What most likely made them? | A leafcutter bee making parcels for its eggs | A hungry caterpillar eating its lunch, bite by bite | A bird pecking for water | Hailstones in the night
test: «there's a massive black bee drilling into the fence, it's gonna eat the whole thing» What is right? | It is a carpenter bee nesting; it pushes the sawdust out and doesn't eat wood | It eats wood like a termite | It is building a honeybee hive inside the fence for the winter | It is sharpening its sting
test: What would happen to tomato flowers in a greenhouse with no bumblebees or other buzzing help? | Much of the pollen would stay locked inside the flower tubes | The tomatoes would grow even bigger without bees bothering them | The flowers would buzz by themselves | Nothing at all would change
test: Why is a queen bumblebee seen in early spring alone, not with a colony? | She slept through winter and now starts a new nest by herself | She was thrown out of the hive by the other bees for being lazy | All the other bees are on holiday | Bumblebees always live alone for life
test: «stingless bees? so they just let anyone rob their honey» What is true? | No, they bite and crowd around intruders | Yes, they have no defence at all, so robbers take it | No, they spray hot liquid | Yes, and they give it away to bears
exam: Identify the reason for which male bees are incapable of stinging. | The sting is a modified egg-laying organ possessed only by females | Male bees shed their stings upon reaching adulthood | Male bees possess a sting too short to penetrate skin | Male bees are forbidden to sting by the queen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mode of life of the majority of bee species? | They are solitary, each female constructing and provisioning her own nest | They inhabit large colonies governed by a single queen and served by many thousands of workers | They live as parasites within the nests of ants | They live in pairs comprising a male and a female
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the supposed proof that bumblebees cannot fly. | It arose from an approximate calculation treating the wing as a rigid aeroplane wing | It was confirmed by careful measurement of flying bees | It applies to bumblebees but not to other bees | It demonstrates that bumblebees glide without flapping
exam: Ascertain the means by which a bumblebee is enabled to fly in cool weather. | It warms its flight muscles by shivering and retains the heat beneath its fur | It absorbs heat exclusively from warm flowers | It consumes heated nectar stored in wax pots within its nest | It flies only when the air exceeds thirty degrees
